d": "A", "text": "It argues that pollination is unrelated to food supplies."}, {"id": "B", "text": "It explains how to keep honeybees in a backyard."}, {"id": "C", "text": "It describes pollinator declines, presents evidence across regions, and explains why pollination matters."}, {"id": "D", "text": "It compares honeybees with hummingbirds in detail."}]'::jsonb, NULL, 'wikipedia-cc-by-sa-4', 'Wikipedia (CC BY-SA 4.0)', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'Wikipedia, "Pollinator decline", CC BY-SA 4.0, retrieved 2026-11-02. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pollinator_decline', FALSE, 'active') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET external_id = EXCLUDED.external_id, assessments = EXCLUDED.assessments, section = EXCLUDED.section, domain = EXCLUDED.domain, skill = EXCLUDED.skill, difficulty = EXCLUDED.difficulty, format = EXCLUDED.format, stimulus = EXCLUDED.stimulus, prompt = EXCLUDED.prompt, choices = EXCLUDED.choices, figure_metadata = EXCLUDED.figure_metadata, source_id = EXCLUDED.source_id, source_name = EXCLUDED.source_name, license = EXCLUDED.license, attribution = EXCLUDED.attribution, is_released_practice_set = EXCLUDED.is_released_practice_set, status = 'active';
INSERT INTO public.question_keys (question_id, correct_answer, acceptable_answers, explanation, distractor_rationales) VALUES ('RW-M22-TS008', 'C', ARRAY['C']::TEXT[], 'The passage moves from the phenomenon to evidence and then to its significance.', '{"B": "No beekeeping advice is given.", "D": "Hummingbirds are not discussed.", "A": "The text calls plants the primary food source for animals and notes pollination''s importance."}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT (question_id) DO UPDATE SET correct_answer = EXCLUDED.correct_answer, acceptable_answers = EXCLUDED.acceptable_answers, explanation = EXCLUDED.explanation, distractor_rationales = EXCLUDED.distractor_rationales;
INSERT INTO public.questions (id, external_id, assessments, section, domain, skill, difficulty, format, stimulus, prompt, choices, figure_metadata, source_id, source_name, license, attribution, is_released_practice_set, status) VALUES ('RW-M22-CE008', 'HDSAT-RW-M22-CE008', ARRAY['SAT', 'PSAT/NMSQT', 'PSAT 10', 'PSAT 8/9']::TEXT[], 'Reading and Writing', 'Information and Ideas', 'Command of Evidence (Textual)', 'Medium', 'multiple_choice', 'Pollinator decline is the reduction in abundance of insect and other animal pollinators in many ecosystems worldwide that began being recorded at the end of the 20th century. Multiple lines of evidence exist for the reduction of wild pollinator populations at the regional level, especially within Europe and North America. Similar findings from studies in South America, China and Japan make it reasonable to suggest that declines are occurring around the globe. The majority of studies focus on bees, particularly honeybee and bumblebee species, with a smaller number involving hoverflies and lepidopterans. The picture for domesticated pollinator species is less clear. Although the number of managed honey bee colonies in Europe and North America declined by 25% and 59% between 1985-2005 and 1947-2005 respectively, overall global stocks increased due to major hive number increases in countries such as China and Argentina. Nevertheless, in the time managed honeybee hives increased by 45% the demand for animal pollinated crops tripled, highlighting the danger of relying on domesticated populations for pollination services. Pollinators participate in the sexual reproduction of many plants by ensuring cross-pollination, essential for some species and a major factor in ensuring genetic diversity for others. Since plants are the primary food source for animals, the possible reduction or disappearance of pollinators has been referred to as an "Armageddon" by some journalists.', 'Which quotation from the text most directly supports the claim that pollinator declines are a global pattern?', '[{"id": "A", "text": "Similar findings from studies in South America, China and Japan make it reasonable to suggest that declines are occurring around the globe."}, {"id": "B", "text": "The majority of studies focus on bees, particularly honeybee and bumblebee species."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Pollinators participate in the sexual reproduction of many plants by ensuring cross-pollination."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Pollinator decline is the reduction in abundance of insect and other animal pollinators in many ecosystems worldwide."}]'::jsonb, NULL, 'wikipedia-cc-by-sa-4', 'Wikipedia (CC BY-SA 4.0)', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'Wikipedia, "Pollinator decline", CC BY-SA 4.0, retrieved 2026-11-02. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pollinator_decline', FALSE, 'active') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET external_id = EXCLUDED.external_id, assessments = EXCLUDED.assessments, section = EXCLUDED.section, domain = EXCLUDED.domain, skill = EXCLUDED.skill, difficulty = EXCLUDED.difficulty, format = EXCLUDED.format, stimulus = EXCLUDED.stimulus, prompt = EXCLUDED.prompt, choices = EXCLUDED.choices, figure_metadata = EXCLUDED.figure_metadata, source_id = EXCLUDED.source_id, source_name = EXCLUDED.source_name, license = EXCLUDED.license, attribution = EXCLUDED.attribution, is_released_practice_set = EXCLUDED.is_released_practice_set, status = 'active';
INSERT INTO public.question_keys (question_id, correct_answer, acceptable_answers, explanation, distractor_rationales) VALUES ('RW-M22-CE008', 'A', ARRAY['A']::TEXT[], 'The correct quotation cites findings on multiple continents as evidence of a global pattern.', '{"D": "This sentence defines pollinator decline.", "B": "This sentence describes which pollinators are studied.", "C": "This sentence explains pollination''s role."}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT (question_id) DO UPDATE SET correct_answer = EXCLUDED.correct_answer, acceptable_answers = EXCLUDED.acceptable_answers, explanation = EXCLUDED.explanation, distractor_rationales = EXCLUDED.distractor_rationales;
INSERT INTO public.questions (id, external_id, assessments, section, domain, skill, difficulty, format, stimulus, prompt, choices, figure_metadata, source_id, source_name, license, attribution, is_released_practice_set, status) VALUES ('RW-M22-CI009', 'HDSAT-RW-M22-CI009', ARRAY['SAT', 'PSAT/NMSQT', 'PSAT 10', 'PSAT 8/9']::TEXT[], 'Reading and Writing', 'Information and Ideas', 'Central Ideas and Details', 'Medium', 'multiple_choice', 'Soil salinity is the salt content in the soil; the process of increasing the salt content is known as salinization (also called salination in American English). Salts occur naturally within soils and water. Salinization can be caused by natural processes such as mineral weathering or by the gradual withdrawal of an ocean. It can also come about through artificial processes such as irrigation and road salt. == Natural occurrence == Salts are a natural component in soils and water bodies. The ions responsible for salinization are: Na+, K+, Ca2+, Mg2+ and Cl−. Over long periods of time, as soil minerals weather and release salts, these salts are flushed or leached out of the soil by drainage water in areas with sufficient precipitation or under irrigation, or may rise through capillarity and accumulate near the surface under intense evaporation during drought periods. In addition to mineral weathering, salts are also deposited via dust and precipitation. Salts may accumulate in dry regions, leading to naturally saline soils. This is the case, for example, in large parts of Australia. Human practices can increase the salinity of soils by the addition of salts in irrigation water. Proper irrigation management can prevent salt accumulation by providing adequate drainage water (e.g. rainwater) to leach added salts from the soil. Disrupting drainage patterns that provide leaching can also result in salt accumulations.', 'Which choice best states the main idea of the text?', '[{"id": "A", "text": "Irrigation always removes salts from soil."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Soil salinity is caused only by ocean flooding."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Soil salinity can arise naturally or from irrigation, and managing drainage can prevent salts from accumulating."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Salts in soil are always harmful and cannot be managed."}]'::jsonb, NULL, 'wikipedia-cc-by-sa-4', 'Wikipedia (CC BY-SA 4.0)', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'Wikipedia, "Soil salinity", CC BY-SA 4.0, retrieved 2026-11-02. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soil_salinity', FALSE, 'active') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET external_id = EXCLUDED.external_id, assessments = EXCLUDED.assessments, section = EXCLUDED.section, domain = EXCLUDED.domain, skill = EXCLUDED.skill, difficulty = EXCLUDED.difficulty, format = EXCLUDED.format, stimulus = EXCLUDED.stimulus, prompt = EXCLUDED.prompt, choices = EXCLUDED.choices, figure_metadata = EXCLUDED.figure_metadata, source_id = EXCLUDED.source_id, source_name = EXCLUDED.source_name, license = EXCLUDED.license, attribution = EXCLUDED.attribution, is_released_practice_set = EXCLUDED.is_released_practice_set, status = 'active';
INSERT INTO public.question_keys (question_id, correct_answer, acceptable_answers, explanation, distractor_rationales) VALUES ('RW-M22-CI009', 'C', ARRAY['C']::TEXT[], 'The passage defines salinity, explains natural and human causes, and describes prevention through drainage.', '{"B": "The text lists mineral weathering, ocean withdrawal, irrigation, and road salt as causes.", "D": "The text says proper irrigation management can prevent salt accumulation.", "A": "The text says irrigation water can add salts; drainage is what leaches them."}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT (question_id) DO UPDATE SET correct_answer = EXCLUDED.correct_answer, acceptable_answers = EXCLUDED.acceptable_answers, explanation = EXCLUDED.explanation, distractor_rationales = EXCLUDED.distractor_rationales;
INSERT INTO public.questions (id, external_id, assessments, section, domain, skill, difficulty, format, stimulus, prompt, choices, figure_metadata, source_id, source_name, license, attribution, is_released_practice_set, status) VALUES ('RW-M22-INF009', 'HDSAT-RW-M22-INF009', ARRAY['SAT', 'PSAT/NMSQT', 'PSAT 10', 'PSAT 8/9']::TEXT[], 'Reading and Writing', 'Information and Ideas', 'Inferences', 'Medium', 'multiple_choice', 'Soil salinity is the salt content in the soil; the process of increasing the salt content is known as salinization (also called salination in American English). Salts occur naturally within soils and water. Salinization can be caused by natural processes such as mineral weathering or by the gradual withdrawal of an ocean. It can also come about through artificial processes such as irrigation and road salt. == Natural occurrence == Salts are a natural component in soils and water bodies. The ions responsible for salinization are: Na+, K+, Ca2+, Mg2+ and Cl−. Over long periods of time, as soil minerals weather and release salts, these salts are flushed or leached out of the soil by drainage water in areas with sufficient precipitation or under irrigation, or may rise through capillarity and accumulate near the surface under intense evaporation during drought periods. In addition to mineral weathering, salts are also deposited via dust and precipitation. Salts may accumulate in dry regions, leading to naturally saline soils. This is the case, for example, in large parts of Australia. Human practices can increase the salinity of soils by the addition of salts in irrigation water. Proper irrigation management can prevent salt accumulation by providing adequate drainage water (e.g. rainwater) to leach added salts from the soil. Disrupting drainage patterns that provide leaching can also result in salt accumulations.
INSERT INTO public.question_keys (question_id, correct_answer, acceptable_answers, explanation, distractor_rationales) VALUES ('RW-M22-INF009', 'B', ARRAY['B']::TEXT[], 'Without drainage to carry salts away, irrigation water leaves salts behind as it evaporates.', '{"A": "The text says disrupting drainage patterns can result in salt accumulations.", "D": "Leaching requires drainage water; without it, salts remain.", "C": "Irrigation does not change regional rainfall."}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT (question_id) DO UPDATE SET correct_answer = EXCLUDED.correct_answer, acceptable_answers = EXCLUDED.acceptable_answers, explanation = EXCLUDED.explanation, distractor_rationales = EXCLUDED.distractor_rationales;
INSERT INTO public.questions (id, external_id, assessments, section, domain, skill, difficulty, format, stimulus, prompt, choices, figure_metadata, source_id, source_name, license, attribution, is_released_practice_set, status) VALUES ('RW-M22-TS009', 'HDSAT-RW-M22-TS009', ARRAY['SAT', 'PSAT/NMSQT', 'PSAT 10', 'PSAT 8/9']::TEXT[], 'Reading and Writing', 'Craft and Structure', 'Text Structure and Purpose', 'Medium', 'multiple_choice', 'Soil salinity is the salt content in the soil; the process of increasing the salt content is known as salinization (also called salination in American English). Salts occur naturally within soils and water. Salinization can be caused by natural processes such as mineral weathering or by the gradual withdrawal of an ocean. It can also come about through artificial processes such as irrigation and road salt. == Natural occurrence == Salts are a natural component in soils and water bodies. The ions responsible for salinization are: Na+, K+, Ca2+, Mg2+ and Cl−. Over long periods of time, as soil minerals weather and release salts, these salts are flushed or leached out of the soil by drainage water in areas with sufficient precipitation or under irrigation, or may rise through capillarity and accumulate near the surface under intense evaporation during drought periods. In addition to mineral weathering, salts are also deposited via dust and precipitation. Salts may accumulate in dry regions, leading to naturally saline soils. This is the case, for example, in large parts of Australia. Human practices can increase the salinity of soils by the addition of salts in irrigation water. Proper irrigation management can prevent salt accumulation by providing adequate drainage water (e.g. rainwater) to leach added salts from the soil. Disrupting drainage patterns that provide leaching can also result in salt accumulations.', 'Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?', '[{"id": "A", "text": "It defines salinization, explains natural causes, and describes how human practices can increase or reduce salinity."}, {"id": "B", "text": "It lists crop species that tolerate salt."}, {"id": "C", "text": "It compares saline soils in Australia with soils on Mars."}, {"id": "D", "text": "It argues that irrigation should be banned."}]'::jsonb, NULL, 'wikipedia-cc-by-sa-4', 'Wikipedia (CC BY-SA 4.0)', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'Wikipedia, "Soil salinity", CC BY-SA 4.0, retrieved 2026-11-02. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soil_salinity', FALSE, 'active') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET external_id = EXCLUDED.external_id, assessments = EXCLUDED.assessments, section = EXCLUDED.section, domain = EXCLUDED.domain, skill = EXCLUDED.skill, difficulty = EXCLUDED.difficulty, format = EXCLUDED.format, stimulus = EXCLUDED.stimulus, prompt = EXCLUDED.prompt, choices = EXCLUDED.choices, figure_metadata = EXCLUDED.figure_metadata, source_id = EXCLUDED.source_id, source_name = EXCLUDED.source_name, license = EXCLUDED.license, attribution = EXCLUDED.attribution, is_released_practice_set = EXCLUDED.is_released_practice_set, status = 'active';
INSERT INTO public.question_keys (question_id, correct_answer, acceptable_answers, explanation, distractor_rationales) VALUES ('RW-M22-TS009', 'A', ARRAY['A']::TEXT[], 'The passage moves from definition to natural processes and then to human management.', '{"C": "Mars is not mentioned.", "B": "No crop species are listed.", "D": "The text discusses irrigation management rather than banning irrigation."}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT (question_id) DO UPDATE SET correct_answer = EXCLUDED.correct_answer, acceptable_answers = EXCLUDED.acceptable_answers, explanation = EXCLUDED.explanation, distractor_rationales = EXCLUDED.distractor_rationales;
INSERT INTO public.questions (id, external_id, assessments, section, domain, skill, difficulty, format, stimulus, prompt, choices, figure_metadata, source_id, source_name, license, attribution, is_released_practice_set, status) VALUES ('RW-M22-CE009', 'HDSAT-RW-M22-CE009', ARRAY['SAT', 'PSAT/NMSQT', 'PSAT 10', 'PSAT 8/9']::TEXT[], 'Reading and Writing', 'Information and Ideas', 'Command of Evidence (Textual)', 'Medium', 'multiple_choice', 'Soil salinity is the salt content in the soil; the process of increasing the salt content is known as salinization (also called salination in American English). Salts occur naturally within soils and water. Salinization can be caused by natural processes such as mineral weathering or by the gradual withdrawal of an ocean. It can also come about through artificial processes such as irrigation and road salt. == Natural occurrence == Salts are a natural component in soils and water bodies. The ions responsible for salinization are: Na+, K+, Ca2+, Mg2+ and Cl−. Over long periods of time, as soil minerals weather and release salts, these salts are flushed or leached out of the soil by drainage water in areas with sufficient precipitation or under irrigation, or may rise through capillarity and accumulate near the surface under intense evaporation during drought periods. In addition to mineral weathering, salts are also deposited via dust and precipitation. Salts may accumulate in dry regions, leading to naturally saline soils. This is the case, for example, in large parts of Australia. Human practices can increase the salinity of soils by the addition of salts in irrigation water. Proper irrigation management can prevent salt accumulation by providing adequate drainage water (e.g. rainwater) to leach added salts from the soil. Disrupting drainage patterns that provide leaching can also result in salt accumulations.', 'Which quotation from the text most directly supports the claim that irrigation can raise soil salinity?', '[{"id": "A", "text": "Salts may accumulate in dry regions, leading to naturally saline soils."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Salts occur naturally within soils and water."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Soil salinity is the salt content in the soil."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Human practices can increase the salinity of soils by the addition of salts in irrigation water."}]'::jsonb, NULL, 'wikipedia-cc-by-sa-4', 'Wikipedia (CC BY-SA 4.0)', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'Wikipedia, "Soil salinity", CC BY-SA 4.0, retrieved 2026-11-02. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soil_salinity', FALSE, 'active') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET external_id = EXCLUDED.external_id, assessments = EXCLUDED.assessments, section = EXCLUDED.section, domain = EXCLUDED.domain, skill = EXCLUDED.skill, difficulty = EXCLUDED.difficulty, format = EXCLUDED.format, stimulus = EXCLUDED.stimulus, prompt = EXCLUDED.prompt, choices = EXCLUDED.choices, figure_metadata = EXCLUDED.figure_metadata, source_id = EXCLUDED.source_id, source_name = EXCLUDED.source_name, license = EXCLUDED.license, attribution = EXCLUDED.attribution, is_released_practice_set = EXCLUDED.is_released_practice_set, status = 'active';
INSERT INTO public.question_keys (question_id, correct_answer, acceptable_answers, explanation, distractor_rationales) VALUES ('RW-M22-CE009', 'D', ARRAY['D']::TEXT[], 'The correct quotation explicitly links irrigation water to increased soil salinity.', '{"C": "This sentence defines soil salinity.", "B": "This sentence notes that salts occur naturally.", "A": "This sentence describes natural accumulation in dry regions."}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT (question_id) DO UPDATE SET correct_answer = EXCLUDED.correct_answer, acceptable_answers = EXCLUDED.acceptable_answers, explanation = EXCLUDED.explanation, distractor_rationales = EXCLUDED.distractor_rationales;
INSERT INTO public.questions (id, external_id, assessments, section, domain, skill, difficulty, format, stimulus, prompt, choices, figure_metadata, source_id, source_name, license, attribution, is_released_practice_set, status) VALUES ('RW-M22-CI010', 'HDSAT-RW-M22-CI010', ARRAY['SAT', 'PSAT/NMSQT', 'PSAT 10', 'PSAT 8/9']::TEXT[], 'Reading and Writing', 'Information and Ideas', 'Central Ideas and Details', 'Medium', 'multiple_choice', 'A mycorrhiza (from Ancient Greek μύκης (múkēs) ''fungus'' and ῥίζα (rhíza) ''root''; pl. mycorrhizae, mycorrhiza, or mycorrhizas) is a symbiotic association between a fungus and a plant, in which fungal hyphae and plant roots become interconnected and form an interface on the cellular level. The term mycorrhiza refers to the role of the fungus in the plant''s rhizosphere, the plant root system and its surroundings. Mycorrhizae play important roles in plant nutrition, soil biology, and soil chemistry. In a mycorrhizal association, the fungus colonizes the host plant''s root tissues, either intracellularly as in arbuscular mycorrhizal fungi, or extracellularly as in ectomycorrhizal fungi. The association is normally mutualistic. In particular species, or in particular circumstances, mycorrhizae may have a parasitic association with host plants. == Definition == A mycorrhiza is a symbiotic association between a green plant and a fungus. The plant makes organic molecules by photosynthesis and supplies them to the fungus in the form of sugars or lipids, while the fungus supplies the plant with water and mineral nutrients, such as phosphorus, nitrogen, or zinc, taken from the soil. Mycorrhizas are located in the roots of vascular plants, but mycorrhiza-like associations also occur in bryophytes and there is fossil evidence that early land plants that lacked roots formed arbuscular mycorrhizal associations.', 'Which choice best states the main idea of the text?', '[{"id": "A", "text": "A mycorrhiza is a usually mutualistic root association in which fungi trade water and nutrients for plant sugars."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Mycorrhizae occur only in plants that lack roots."}, {"id": "C", "text": "In a mycorrhiza, the plant supplies the fungus with minerals."}, {"id": "D", "text": "A mycorrhiza is a disease that destroys plant roots."}]'::jsonb, NULL, 'wikipedia-cc-by-sa-4', 'Wikipedia (CC BY-SA 4.0)', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'Wikipedia, "Mycorrhiza", CC BY-SA 4.0, retrieved 2026-11-02. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mycorrhiza', FALSE, 'active') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET external_id = EXCLUDED.external_id, assessments = EXCLUDED.assessments, section = EXCLUDED.section, domain = EXCLUDED.domain, skill = EXCLUDED.skill, difficulty = EXCLUDED.difficulty, format = EXCLUDED.format, stimulus = EXCLUDED.stimulus, prompt = EXCLUDED.prompt, choices = EXCLUDED.choices, figure_metadata = EXCLUDED.figure_metadata, source_id = EXCLUDED.source_id, source_name = EXCLUDED.source_name, license = EXCLUDED.license, attribution = EXCLUDED.attribution, is_released_practice_set = EXCLUDED.is_released_practice_set, status = 'active';
INSERT INTO public.question_keys (question_id, correct_answer, acceptable_answers, explanation, distractor_rationales) VALUES ('RW-M22-CI010', 'A', ARRAY['A']::TEXT[], 'The passage defines mycorrhizae, explains the exchange between fungus and plant, and notes exceptions.', '{"D": "The text says the association is normally mutualistic, though sometimes parasitic.", "C": "The text says the fungus supplies the plant with water and mineral nutrients.", "B": "The text says mycorrhizas are located in the roots of vascular plants."}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT (question_id) DO UPDATE SET correct_answer = EXCLUDED.correct_answer, acceptable_answers = EXCLUDED.acceptable_answers, explanation = EXCLUDED.explanation, distractor_rationales = EXCLUDED.distractor_rationales;
INSERT INTO public.questions (id, external_id, assessments, section, domain, skill, difficulty, format, stimulus, prompt, choices, figure_metadata, source_id, source_name, license, attribution, is_released_practice_set, status) VALUES ('RW-M22-INF010', 'HDSAT-RW-M22-INF010', ARRAY['SAT', 'PSAT/NMSQT', 'PSAT 10', 'PSAT 8/9']::TEXT[], 'Reading and Writing', 'Information and Ideas', 'Inferences', 'Medium', 'multiple_choice', 'A mycorrhiza (from Ancient Greek μύκης (múkēs) ''fungus'' and ῥίζα (rhíza) ''root''; pl. mycorrhizae, mycorrhiza, or mycorrhizas) is a symbiotic association between a fungus and a plant, in which fungal hyphae and plant roots become interconnected and form an interface on the cellular level. The term mycorrhiza refers to the role of the fungus in the plant''s rhizosphere, the plant root system and its surroundings. Mycorrhizae play important roles in plant nutrition, soil biology, and soil chemistry. In a mycorrhizal association, the fungus colonizes the host plant''s root tissues, either intracellularly as in arbuscular mycorrhizal fungi, or extracellularly as in ectomycorrhizal fungi. The association is normally mutualistic. In particular species, or in particular circumstances, mycorrhizae may have a parasitic association with host plants. == Definition == A mycorrhiza is a symbiotic association between a green plant and a fungus. The plant makes organic molecules by photosynthesis and supplies them to the fungus in the form of sugars or lipids, while the fungus supplies the plant with water and mineral nutrients, such as phosphorus, nitrogen, or zinc, taken from the soil. Mycorrhizas are located in the roots of vascular plants, but mycorrhiza-like associations also occur in bryophytes and there is fossil evidence that early land plants that lacked roots formed arbuscular mycorrhizal associations.
INSERT INTO public.question_keys (question_id, correct_answer, acceptable_answers, explanation, distractor_rationales) VALUES ('RW-M22-INF010', 'D', ARRAY['D']::TEXT[], 'Fungal hyphae deliver soil nutrients to the plant, supplementing what roots take up directly.', '{"C": "The fungi extend the plant''s access to soil nutrients.", "A": "The text says the plant makes organic molecules by photosynthesis.", "B": "The text says the plant supplies sugars or lipids to the fungus, so it still produces them."}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT (question_id) DO UPDATE SET correct_answer = EXCLUDED.correct_answer, acceptable_answers = EXCLUDED.acceptable_answers, explanation = EXCLUDED.explanation, distractor_rationales = EXCLUDED.distractor_rationales;
INSERT INTO public.questions (id, external_id, assessments, section, domain, skill, difficulty, format, stimulus, prompt, choices, figure_metadata, source_id, source_name, license, attribution, is_released_practice_set, status) VALUES ('RW-M22-TS010', 'HDSAT-RW-M22-TS010', ARRAY['SAT', 'PSAT/NMSQT', 'PSAT 10', 'PSAT 8/9']::TEXT[], 'Reading and Writing', 'Craft and Structure', 'Text Structure and Purpose', 'Medium', 'multiple_choice', 'A mycorrhiza (from Ancient Greek μύκης (múkēs) ''fungus'' and ῥίζα (rhíza) ''root''; pl. mycorrhizae, mycorrhiza, or mycorrhizas) is a symbiotic association between a fungus and a plant, in which fungal hyphae and plant roots become interconnected and form an interface on the cellular level. The term mycorrhiza refers to the role of the fungus in the plant''s rhizosphere, the plant root system and its surroundings. Mycorrhizae play important roles in plant nutrition, soil biology, and soil chemistry. In a mycorrhizal association, the fungus colonizes the host plant''s root tissues, either intracellularly as in arbuscular mycorrhizal fungi, or extracellularly as in ectomycorrhizal fungi. The association is normally mutualistic. In particular species, or in particular circumstances, mycorrhizae may have a parasitic association with host plants. == Definition == A mycorrhiza is a symbiotic association between a green plant and a fungus. The plant makes organic molecules by photosynthesis and supplies them to the fungus in the form of sugars or lipids, while the fungus supplies the plant with water and mineral nutrients, such as phosphorus, nitrogen, or zinc, taken from the soil. Mycorrhizas are located in the roots of vascular plants, but mycorrhiza-like associations also occur in bryophytes and there is fossil evidence that early land plants that lacked roots formed arbuscular mycorrhizal associations.', 'Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?', '[{"id": "A", "text": "It explains how to inoculate a garden with fungi."}, {"id": "B", "text": "It compares mycorrhizae with lichens in detail."}, {"id": "C", "text": "It defines mycorrhiza, describes the exchange between plant and fungus, and notes where the association occurs."}, {"id": "D", "text": "It argues that all fungi are parasites."}]'::jsonb, NULL, 'wikipedia-cc-by-sa-4', 'Wikipedia (CC BY-SA 4.0)', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'Wikipedia, "Mycorrhiza", CC BY-SA 4.0, retrieved 2026-11-02. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mycorrhiza', FALSE, 'active') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET external_id = EXCLUDED.external_id, assessments = EXCLUDED.assessments, section = EXCLUDED.section, domain = EXCLUDED.domain, skill = EXCLUDED.skill, difficulty = EXCLUDED.difficulty, format = EXCLUDED.format, stimulus = EXCLUDED.stimulus, prompt = EXCLUDED.prompt, choices = EXCLUDED.choices, figure_metadata = EXCLUDED.figure_metadata, source_id = EXCLUDED.source_id, source_name = EXCLUDED.source_name, license = EXCLUDED.license, attribution = EXCLUDED.attribution, is_released_practice_set = EXCLUDED.is_released_practice_set, status = 'active';
INSERT INTO public.question_keys (question_id, correct_answer, acceptable_answers, explanation, distractor_rationales) VALUES ('RW-M22-TS010', 'C', ARRAY['C']::TEXT[], 'The passage moves from definition to exchange and distribution.', '{"A": "No inoculation instructions are given.", "B": "Lichens are not mentioned.", "D": "The text says the association is normally mutualistic and only sometimes parasitic."}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT (question_id) DO UPDATE SET correct_answer = EXCLUDED.correct_answer, acceptable_answers = EXCLUDED.acceptable_answers, explanation = EXCLUDED.explanation, distractor_rationales = EXCLUDED.distractor_rationales;
INSERT INTO public.questions (id, external_id, assessments, section, domain, skill, difficulty, format, stimulus, prompt, choices, figure_metadata, source_id, source_name, license, attribution, is_released_practice_set, status) VALUES ('RW-M22-CE010', 'HDSAT-RW-M22-CE010', ARRAY['SAT', 'PSAT/NMSQT', 'PSAT 10', 'PSAT 8/9']::TEXT[], 'Reading and Writing', 'Information and Ideas', 'Command of Evidence (Textual)', 'Medium', 'multiple_choice', 'A mycorrhiza (from Ancient Greek μύκης (múkēs) ''fungus'' and ῥίζα (rhíza) ''root''; pl. mycorrhizae, mycorrhiza, or mycorrhizas) is a symbiotic association between a fungus and a plant, in which fungal hyphae and plant roots become interconnected and form an interface on the cellular level. The term mycorrhiza refers to the role of the fungus in the plant''s rhizosphere, the plant root system and its surroundings. Mycorrhizae play important roles in plant nutrition, soil biology, and soil chemistry. In a mycorrhizal association, the fungus colonizes the host plant''s root tissues, either intracellularly as in arbuscular mycorrhizal fungi, or extracellularly as in ectomycorrhizal fungi. The association is normally mutualistic. In particular species, or in particular circumstances, mycorrhizae may have a parasitic association with host plants. == Definition == A mycorrhiza is a symbiotic association between a green plant and a fungus. The plant makes organic molecules by photosynthesis and supplies them to the fungus in the form of sugars or lipids, while the fungus supplies the plant with water and mineral nutrients, such as phosphorus, nitrogen, or zinc, taken from the soil. Mycorrhizas are located in the roots of vascular plants, but mycorrhiza-like associations also occur in bryophytes and there is fossil evidence that early land plants that lacked roots formed arbuscular mycorrhizal associations.', 'Which quotation from the text most directly supports the claim that both partners benefit in a mycorrhiza?', '[{"id": "A", "text": "A mycorrhiza is a symbiotic association between a fungus and a plant."}, {"id": "B", "text": "In particular species, or in particular circumstances, mycorrhizae may have a parasitic association with host plants."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Mycorrhizas are located in the roots of vascular plants."}, {"id": "D", "text": "The plant makes organic molecules by photosynthesis and supplies them to the fungus in the form of sugars or lipids, while the fungus supplies the plant with water and mineral nutrients, such as phosphorus, nitrogen, or zinc, taken from the soil."}]'::jsonb, NULL, 'wikipedia-cc-by-sa-4', 'Wikipedia (CC BY-SA 4.0)', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'Wikipedia, "Mycorrhiza", CC BY-SA 4.0, retrieved 2026-11-02. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mycorrhiza', FALSE, 'active') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET external_id = EXCLUDED.external_id, assessments = EXCLUDED.assessments, section = EXCLUDED.section, domain = EXCLUDED.domain, skill = EXCLUDED.skill, difficulty = EXCLUDED.difficulty, format = EXCLUDED.format, stimulus = EXCLUDED.stimulus, prompt = EXCLUDED.prompt, choices = EXCLUDED.choices, figure_metadata = EXCLUDED.figure_metadata, source_id = EXCLUDED.source_id, source_name = EXCLUDED.source_name, license = EXCLUDED.license, attribution = EXCLUDED.attribution, is_released_practice_set = EXCLUDED.is_released_practice_set, status = 'active';
